Merge compatible import declarations for the same module, including declarations separated by imports from other modules:

```js
import { b } from "./res-SWvxTGCX.js";
import { g } from "./res-SWvxTGCX.js";
// becomes
import { b, g } from "./res-SWvxTGCX.js";
```

Preserve separate declarations when import attributes, phases, type/value modes, or namespace specifiers prevent merging. Combine multiple default bindings using named `default` imports. Keep source-phase imports separate because their syntax permits only one binding; a regression test checks that they remain parseable and idempotent.

TS1265 and TS1266 depend on the resolved types of tuple rest elements. The parser cannot reliably distinguish concrete arrays from tuple aliases or variadic type parameters. Its spelling-based `Array` check rejects valid code such as:

```ts
// Array shadows the built-in name and is a variadic type parameter.
// Both are valid, but the parser incorrectly reported TS1265 / TS1266.
type X<Array extends unknown[]> = [...Array, ...Array];
type Y<Array extends unknown[]> = [...Array, number?];
```

```ts
type Pair = [string, number];
type Items = string[];

// Valid: spreading Pair contributes two fixed elements, then an optional one.
type Fixed = [...Pair, boolean?];

// Invalid: Items is an unbounded array rest, but the parser cannot resolve it.
type OptionalAfterArray = [...Items, boolean?]; // TS1266
// Invalid: two unbounded array rests; also requires resolving the alias.
type TwoArrays = [...Items, ...Items]; // TS1265
```

```ts
// Invalid: the unconstrained type parameter might not be an array.
// TypeScript reports TS2574, but the parser incorrectly reported TS1265.
type Unconstrained<Array> = [...Array, ...Array];

// Invalid: the built-in Array requires a type argument.
// TypeScript reports TS2314, but the parser incorrectly reported TS1265.
type MissingArguments = [...Array, ...Array];
```

